racerand uses only the Go standard library.

Statistical definitions and numerical formulas are based on these references:

- NIST SP 800-90B, Recommendation for the Entropy Sources Used for Random
  Bit Generation (2018): https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.SP.800-90B
- NIST SP 800-22 Rev. 1a, A Statistical Test Suite for Random and Pseudorandom
  Number Generators for Cryptographic Applications (2010):
  https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.SP.800-22r1a
- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, sections 8.7 and 8.9:
  https://dlmf.nist.gov/8.7 and https://dlmf.nist.gov/8.9
- John Walker's ent describes the distribution diagnostics used for comparison:
  https://www.fourmilab.ch/random/

These references do not imply endorsement, validation, or certification.
Jitterentropy, HAVEGE, and other projects cited in the README are related work;
their implementations are not bundled or benchmarked here.
